course: dictionary
lesson: 436
title: Meet the archaeologist
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An archaeologist is a scientist who studies how people lived long ago. They do this by digging up and examining old things people left behind, such as bones, tools, pots and buildings. Look for the clues in the word itself: archaeologists dig, and they study old things.
explain: Archaeologists dig very carefully. They record where each find was, because the place a thing was found tells us how people used it. They also work out how old it is. «Archaeologists (people who dig up and study old remains) dig up the real clues.» The brackets in that sentence explain the word for you.
explain: Archaeologists study remains of people and things people made. Dinosaur bones are studied by palaeontologists instead, so do not mix up the two jobs. Archaeologists can help us learn about the Stone Age, the Romans, the Vikings and the Egyptians.
explain: Be careful with big claims. «Some old dates are traditions rather than measurements.» A tradition is a story passed down over time, and it may not be true. Archaeologists look for evidence, such as tools, homes and bones, before they believe a claim.
fact: An archaeologist is a scientist who studies how people lived long ago.
fact: Archaeologists dig up and examine old things, such as bones, tools, pots and buildings.
fact: Archaeologists record where each find was and work out its age.
fact: Dinosaur bones are studied by palaeontologists, not archaeologists.
fact: A «tradition» is a story or belief passed down over time, so it needs evidence before we trust it.
exercise: What does an archaeologist study? | Stars and planets | *How people lived long ago, from the things they left behind | Plants that grow in the sea | The weather of the future
exercise: Which of these would an archaeologist most likely dig up? | A new car | Rain from a storm | *Stone Age tools | A cloud in the sky
exercise: Why do archaeologists record where each find was? | To sell it to a shop | To hide it from other people | *To help work out the story of the find | To make the find look shiny
test: «Archaeologists have found the tools, homes and bones of the people who really built Stonehenge.» What do these finds prove? | Stonehenge is made of gold | Aliens built Stonehenge | Stonehenge was never built | *Real people built Stonehenge
test: «The historian Sima Qian wrote that the emperor's tomb has rivers of mercury.» Why have archaeologists not opened the main tomb? | *Mainly to protect it | They are too busy to dig | It is full of water | They lost the key
exam: Archaeologists and palaeontologists both study old things. Who studies dinosaur bones? | *Palaeontologists | Historians | Archaeologists | Dinosaur keepers
